WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:11.430 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  the team behind marvel's midnight suns  are best known for xcom the stellar  tactical strategy series about sending  squads of elite soldiers against an  invading alien force and often watching  those soldiers die horrifically the  logical pitch for midnight suns then is  x-com but with superheroes but in fact  the new marvel title from developer  faraxis games is much more rpg than  strategy game this isn't another xcom  it's something very different as for  raxis notes midnight suns focuses on  some of the more spooky elements of the  marvel universe including its more  arcane heroes the initial premise is  pretty standard the evil military group  hydra uses some occult weirdness to  resurrect a supervillain named lilith  the mother of demons and starts aiding  her and taking over the world to combat  that threat characters like doctor  strange and iron man band together with  more supernaturally inclined folks from  around the marvel universe such as blade  ghost rider and magic included in that  group is the hunter a fully customizable  character you create who is also  lilith's child and the chosen one who  can stop her once and for all but first  let's talk about midnight sun's approach  to tactical combat back when it first  started showing off the game paraxis  revealed that it uses card based deck  building mechanics for its battle system  each character in your combat team of  three has a handful of cards that  represent their abilities and when you  start your turn in a battle you draw a  hand of random cards you can play a set  number of cards per turn so which ones  you draw determine what your characters  can do it's a system that seems  drastically different from the tactical  approach that xcom players are used to  in the xcom games each character you use  has a set of actions or abilities they  can use each turn but each of those  abilities has a percentage attached to  it that tells you its chances of hitting  or missing as creative director jake  solomon explained during our play  session though the idea of characters  missing their attacks or their attacks  having a chance to do low damage broke  the fantasy of controlling an awesome  superhero team but some degree of  randomness is essential for making  tactical games fun and unpredictable  farax's solution was to move that  randomness to character abilities  forcing you to think carefully about  which cards to play when and in what  order to maximize your team's  effectiveness your abilities come with a  lot of caveats and additional effects  making team synergy and careful planning  extremely important for instance some  attack cards come with the knockback  effect where the enemy you hit can be  sent flying if you crash them into an  object you can deal extra damage send  them into another character and you can  hurt them both quick attacks give you an  extra card play in your turn if you kill  the enemy you're attacking since you can  only play three cards in a turn having  quick cards can extend your actions  significantly you also need to think  about which enemies you target since  your cards will automatically move your  characters around the battlefield which  is sometimes essential to keeping them  out of harm's way from a coming attack  or effect playing cards also builds up a  resource called heroism which is another  key element of your planning your more  powerful cards require you to spend  heroism to use them so you want to build  up your stock for those big moments  where you can do things like drive ghost  rider's bike through multiple enemies or  bombard the battlefield with iron man's  missiles but you can also use heroism  without cards spending heroism can allow  your characters to interact with the  environment like throwing a rock at an  enemy or blowing up an explosive barrel  if you've got enough heroism the  environment can give you what are  essentially additional attack  opportunities it all works together to  create a different tactical feel from  x-com thinking through your options and  abilities is often about clearing out as  many enemies as you can in one go or  knocking out the enemies focusing their  attacks on one of your squad members in  order to reduce the amount of damage  they'll take next turn you need to think  about controlling your positioning  clearing out important enemy threats  building heroism to execute big  abilities and more it creates a puzzle  that is similar to xcom but also feels  completely different in the moment the  card system introduces randomness into  battles that makes them feel variable  and interesting decks are relatively  small and easily tuned and you have the  opportunity to redraw specific cards  each turn so you never feel like you're  getting beaten by a bad hand from the  rng the characters you bring into fights  also add a lot of variety to your  tactical considerations because of their  different abilities and roles blade for  instance is a damage focused character  great for taking out tough enemies  because he can inflict the bleed status  effect that causes them to lose health  over time doctor strange can boost his  teammates and in so doing his attacks  become more powerful you can also adjust  each character's deck as you earn more  cards through midnight sun's crafting  system allowing you to focus on specific  aspects of their abilities but each  character's deck never has more than  about 8 cards in it so it never gets too  complicated farax's also made a big deal  of the hunter the hunter has a variety  of abilities that span from straight  damage dealing to support and healing as  well as an alignment meter that ranges  from light to dark the hunter's  alignment changes depending on the cards  you play support cards tend to be light  damage cards tend to be dark which  changes their effectiveness so the  character reacts to how you tune your  hunter's deck and according to your  playstyle becoming more powerful in your  dedicated area and if you choose to  generalize taking on abilities from all  over the spectrum your alignment  reflects that too the alignment system  isn't just about battles and cards  though it's an rpg system that keeps  track of your choices and dialogue as  you work through the story and interact  with members of your team as the hunter  and there's a surprisingly huge amount  of dialogue to work with midnight suns  tracks the flow of time in a way that's  similar to xcom limiting the actions you  can take during each in-game day at the  start of a day you wake up in the abbey  the midnight sun's base of operations  where you can run around having  conversations with characters chasing  down collectibles and advancing story  beats and interacting with the crafting  system that can earn you new cards at  some point you'll eventually pick a  combat mission to take on the further in  the game you get like x-com the more  missions become available and you can  choose to take on key story battles or  go after more generic ones that offer  resources you might need after your  mission for the day you return to the  abbey at night where more story unfolds  in fact a huge part of the five hours we  played were dedicated to walking around  as the hunter in the abbey learning  about the various elements of the game  there are a lot of tutorials strung  together as part of the narrative in the  early part of the game as midnight suns  gives you the lowdown on combat dialogue  mechanics team building elements such as  training to boost your card's  effectiveness crafting and more but the  game doesn't seem to have more systems  than you'd expect from say the base  building side of xcom what was  surprising was how much of those  tutorials are built into the  storytelling and character interaction  during the session solomon said that the  midnight sun's team kept finding that  they wanted to spend more time with the  characters the draw for them was as much  about the personalities of the  superheroes and their friendships and  rivalries as it was about using them in  battle alongside conversations you can  have with characters as the story  progresses which come with a variety of  dialogue options the nighttime portions  of each day give you opportunities to  hang out with one specific character all  that time with your team plays into the  friendship system which allows you to  unlock better ability cards and more  synergy with your teammates based on how  close your relationship with them is it  incentivizes hanging around with the  other superheroes as well as learning  about them blade for instance isn't a  fan of your snarky jokes while magic  appreciates it if you can relate to the  darker elements of her past phyraxis  said the dialogue options won't change  the flow of the narrative but it  definitely will affect your  relationships with different characters  and you can only choose one character to  hang out with for a given day so your  choices for dealing with other  characters affect both how your  particular story plays out as you learn  more about each person and some heroes  more than others and how your team  functions in battle the tone feels a bit  more in line with something like the  avengers films or the guardians of the  galaxy with plenty of quips and humor  between the characters and tons of  references to the marvel universe  tony that's uh carol and dr spooky over  there  here to help somewhat coincidentally the  game's story actually has a lot in  common with a recently released movie  doctor strange and the multiverse of  madness despite fibraxis beginning the  development of its tale some five years  ago expect to see a big cross-section of  the marvel universe take part in the  game as well the portion we played  included missions with spider-man on the  team fighting a demonic version of venom  who had been possessed by lilith  marvel's midnight sons was surprising in  that while it looks a bit like the x-com  games it tends to feel drastically  different from them it's also a major  departure from phyraxis usual offerings  with an increased focus on storytelling  and rpg elements that go far beyond the  combat at its center but while midnight  suns is a little out of phyraxis  wheelhouse it was also fun to play its  battle system puts a big spin on the  underlying ideas of tactical turn-based  combat that also feels more approachable  than the slightly more hardcore focus of  xcom although we also saw that midnight  suns will offer a bunch of high  difficulty options so you can really  challenge yourself and it's storytelling  though dense puts a lot of emphasis on a  group of deep and interesting characters  over the course of 60 hours it seems  there will be a whole lot to explore in  midnight suns and from what we've seen  so far  uncovering secrets building  relationships and mastering combat  mechanics are going to be rewarding for  strategy and rpg fans alike how about i  take eddie here for his afternoon walk  don't worry doc that's just his way of  saying he cares

